<chapter
 id="lecture09">
#title("Raising and Catching Exceptions")

#para("\

Exceptions provide a mechanism for altering the (normal) control-flow
during program execution. Raising an exception is somewhat like
executing a goto-statement.  A handler for handling a raised exception
is essentially a pattern matching clause guarded by a pattern (for
matching exceptions).  Intuitively speaking, a raised exception passes
through a stack of handlers; the raised exception is handled by a
handler if the exception matches the guard of the handler, or it
simply tries the next handler to see if it can be handled; the raised
exception terminates program execution abnormally if it is not handled
by any of the handlers.

")

#para("\

In ATS, a try-expression (or try-with-expression) is of the form
(#keycode("try") exp #keycode("with") clseq), where #keycode("try") is
a keyword, exp is an expression, #keycode("with") is also a keyword,
and clseq is a sequence of pattern matching clauses (used as
exception-handlers). When evaluating such a try-expression, we first
evaluate exp. If the evaluation of exp leads to a value, then the
value is also the value of the try-expression. If the evaluation of
exp leads to a raised exception, then we match the exception against
the guards of the matching clauses in clseq. If there is a match, the
raised exception is caught and we continue to evaluate the body of the
first clause whose guard is matched. If there is no match, the raised
exception is uncaught. In the following example,
#dyncode("int_exists") is implemented based on
#dyncode("int_foreach") of the chapter on higher-order functions
(it tells whether some integer less than n satisfies a given test):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// Stopping a loop by raising an exception
//
fun
int_exists
(n: int, test: (int) -> bool): bool = let
//
exception True of ()
//
in
//
try let
//
var fwork =
lam@(i: int): void => if test(i) then $raise True()
//
val () = int_foreach(n, fwork)
//
in
  false
end with ~True() => true
//
end // end of [int_exists]
//
val () = println! ("exists i < 10. i*i = 49: ", int_exists(10, lam(i) => i*i = 49))
val () = println! ("exists i < 5. i*i = 49: ", int_exists(5, lam(i) => i*i = 49))
//
')

Given a natural number and a predicate, #dyncode("int_exists") returns a
boolean value to indicate whether there exists a natural number less than the given one
that satisfies the predicate.  There is a built-in type
#stacode("exn") in ATS, which is somewhat like an extensible datatype
in the sense that a new constructor associated with #stacode("exn")
can be introduced through an exception-declaration. For instance,
#dyncode("True") is introduced as a nullary exception-constructor in
the body of #dyncode("int_exists"). Given a natural number and a function,
#dyncode("int_foreach") normally traverses until the end while appying the
function to each encountered integer.  When the call
to #dyncode("int_foreach") in the body of #dyncode("int_exists")
is evaluated, an exception (#dyncode("True()")) is raised to stop
further traversing if an integer satisfying the predicate
#dyncode("test") is encountered.  If the call to
#dyncode("int_foreach") returns, then the value #dyncode("false") is
the return value of #dyncode("int_exists").  If #dyncode("True()")
is raised during the evaluation of the call, then this raised
exception is to be caught by the handler following the keyword
#keycode("with"), resulting in the value #dyncode("true") becoming the
return value of #dyncode("int_exists").  Note that a raised
exception is considered a resource in ATS (it is linear) and it needs to be properly
freed or re-raised after being caught. The symbol #dyncode("~") in the
pattern #dyncode("~True()") indicates that the caught execption is
freed.

")

#para("\

There is a restriction that comes with the absence of GC: raising an
exception abandons whatever linear value is held by the function calls that the
exception passes through (they are not resumed, and nobody frees what they hold). Therefore, the
typechecker does not permit a linear value (a list, an array, a closure on heap) to be used in the
body of a try-expression unless the value is created in it. This is why
the example above is about integers rather than about the linear lists of the
earlier chapters (the GC-era version of this chapter implemented
#dyncode("list0_exists") with an exception raised by the closure passed to
#dyncode("list0_foreach")): for a list, the loop that stops when it finds an element
is a recursion that returns (see #dyncode("list_find_index")), and an exception is
used for data that is not linear. The flat closure #dyncode("fwork") above is fine
since it is created in the try-expression.

")

#para("\

By raising an exception, a function can efficiently pass some
information gathered during its evaluation. Let us see a typical
example of this kind as follows. Binary trees are an example of shared data
(a subtree can be shared by many trees), which are kept in an arena by the
program: the type #stacode("tree") is the type of the handles of the nodes, and
it is not linear. The file #filename("tree.spec") in the code of this chapter
specifies the type for the tool #command("arenagen") (see the chapter on the
game of 24):

<informalexample><programlisting>
type tree
  TREEnil ()
  TREEcons (tree, int, tree)
</programlisting></informalexample>

The generated interface is in #filename("tree.sats"). For instance, the
function for computing the height of a given
binary tree can be implemented as follows (the function
#dyncode("tree_view") turns a handle into a value of a linear datavtype that
is used for pattern matching and consumed by the patterns with the
symbol #keycode("~")):

#dats2xhtml('\
//
fun
tree_height(t0: tree): int =
(
case+ tree_view(t0) of
| ~TREEnil() => 0
| ~TREEcons(tl, _, tr) =>
  1 + max(tree_height(tl), tree_height(tr))
)
//
')

Note that #dyncode("tree_height") is O(n)-time, where n is the size of
its argument.  A binary is perfect if it is empty or both of its
children are perfect and of the same height. For instance, the
following function #dyncode("tree_is_perfect") checks whether a given
binary tree is perfect:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
fun
tree_is_perfect(t0: tree): bool =
(
case+ tree_view(t0) of
| ~TREEnil() => true
| ~TREEcons(tl, _, tr) =>
  tree_is_perfect(tl) &&
  tree_is_perfect(tr) &&
  (tree_height(tl) = tree_height(tr))
)
//
')

Clearly, the time complexity of #dyncode("tree_is_perfect") is
O(n(log(n))) where n is the size of its argument. By making use of
exception, we can readily implement the following function of
O(n)-time that also checks whether a given tree is perfect:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
fun
tree_is_perfect2
  (t0: tree): bool = let
//
exception NotPerfect of ()
//
fun
aux(t0: tree): int =
case+ tree_view(t0) of
| ~TREEnil() => 0
| ~TREEcons(tl, _, tr) => let
    val hl = aux(tl) and hr = aux(tr)
  in
    if hl = hr
      then 1+max(hl, hr) else $raise NotPerfect()
    // end of [if]
  end
//
in
  try let val _ = aux(t0) in true end with ~NotPerfect() => false
end // end of [tree_is_perfect2]
//
')

The inner function #dyncode("aux") inside #dyncode("tree_is_perfect2")
returns the height of a given tree only if the tree is
perfect. Otherwise, an exception (#dyncode("NotPerfect()")) is
raised. In other words, if a call to #dyncode("aux") on a tree
returns, we know that the tree is pefect while obtaining its height.
This is often dubbed a #emphasis("hitting-two-birds-with-one-stone")
scenario. The following code builds a perfect tree of height 10, in
which the two subtrees of a node are the same node, and tests the two functions:

#dats2xhtml('\
//
// the perfect tree of a given height; the subtrees are shared
//
fun
tree_perfect(h: int): tree =
if h <= 0
  then tree_TREEnil()
  else let
    val t = tree_perfect(h-1) in tree_TREEcons(t, h, t)
  end // end of [else]
//
val perfect = tree_perfect(10)
val unperfect = tree_TREEcons(perfect, 0, tree_TREEnil())
//
val () = println! ("height(perfect) = ", tree_height(perfect))
val () = println! ("is_perfect(perfect) = ", tree_is_perfect(perfect))
val () = println! ("is_perfect2(perfect) = ", tree_is_perfect2(perfect))
val () = println! ("is_perfect(unperfect) = ", tree_is_perfect(unperfect))
val () = println! ("is_perfect2(unperfect) = ", tree_is_perfect2(unperfect))
//
')

")

#para("\

Please find #mycodelink("LECTURE/09/CODE", "on-line") the entirety of
the code used in this chapter. It was compiled with #command("patscc")
(no flag is needed) and run with the address sanitizer, which reports no
leak. \

")#comment("para")

</chapter><!--id="lecture09"-->

